Task 2: Create Groups and Add Members

The second task focused on group-based access management.

Groups help administrators organize users and assign permissions efficiently.

Understanding Group Membership Types

The lab explained two group membership methods:

Assigned Membership

Administrators manually add or remove members.

Dynamic Membership

Membership automatically updates based on user properties such as:

Department
Job title
Location

Dynamic membership helps reduce administrative overhead in large environments.

Creating a Security Group

A security group named:

IT Lab Administrators

was created with assigned membership.

The group configuration included:

Setting	Value
Group Type	Security
Membership Type	Assigned
Description	Administrators that manage the IT lab

The lab also demonstrated assigning:

Group owners
Internal users
Guest users

to the security group.

Key Governance Features Explored

The lab introduced additional group governance settings:

Group expiration policies
Naming policies
Blocked words
Prefix and suffix enforcement

These governance features help organizations standardize identity management and improve security practices.

Important Azure Identity Concepts Learned
Microsoft Entra ID

Microsoft Entra ID provides:

Identity management
Authentication services
Access control
User and group administration
Secure collaboration
Security Groups

Security groups simplify permission management by assigning access to groups instead of individual users.

Benefits include:

Easier administration
Centralized permission management
Scalability
Improved security management
Guest Users

Guest accounts allow external users to securely collaborate inside an Azure environment without requiring full internal accounts.

Dynamic Group Membership

Dynamic membership automatically updates group membership based on rules and user attributes.

Example conditions:

Job title equals IT Administrator
Department equals IT

This automation is valuable in enterprise environments with large numbers of users.

